/usr/bin/compare from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for compare, so this is the disassembly, and compare.c is
that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f compare.ctl compare.dist

compare.dist is the binary off inode 61 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 7858
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 1df2 and c.ents at
1a56.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's Software Tools.

Its own code is main at 0152 and seven small functions, with the strings in
front of it: "eof on file 2." at 010e, "eof on file 1." at 011d, the modes
"read" at 012c and 0131, the usage line at 0136 and ": can't open." at 0309.

main wants three arguments, a name and two files, and says "usage: compare
file1 file2." on the standard error if it has fewer.  It opens both with the
library's fopen (0426), "name: can't open." for one that will not, and goes round
a loop: a line from each with fgets (085e, handed 512), and, unless either has
met the end of its file, the line number is counted up and the lines compared
with the library's string compare; if they differ differ (0359) writes the
number and a newline and the two lines, as they were read, to the standard
output.  Each line has the newline it was read with, so what a difference looks
like is

    2
    b
    X

and a last line with no newline runs into whatever is written next.  Files that
are the same write nothing at all.

TWO THINGS THE BINARY HAS AND DOES NOT USE

The two "eof on file" messages are printed from 02c6, when the loop ends, by two
tests on the words at fp-based offsets FBF6 and FBF8 of main's frame.  Nothing
in main stores to them - the compiler left them where it found them, it seems,
or the stores were lost - and so the tests are on whatever the stack held.  On a
clear stack both are 0 and neither test passes, which is what usersim has, and
neither message is ever written.  The intention is plain: FBF8 is the result of
the read from the first file and FBF6 of the one from the second, and a message
says which file ended while the other had a line.  compare.c does not guess at
it; it writes nothing, which is what the original does wherever it has been run
here.

The end of the loop is the end of either file met, in the middle of a line or at
the end of one, so a last line that has no newline - which sets the flag as it
is read - is not compared; the same behaviour as include's.  Lines are 511
characters, newline included, and a longer line is two.

The exit status was 1 for a run that finished and 0 for the usage line and for a
file that would not open.  compare.c exits 0 and 1.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, standard error and all:
two files the same, two with thirty of two hundred lines different, each way
round; one shorter than the other, each way round; a last line with no newline in
one, in both, and in neither; two files of two lines of 600 characters; an empty
file against a full one and against another; no arguments, one argument, three
arguments, and a file that does not exist in each place.
